    35	
    36	## 2. The canonicalization operator (the treatment)
    37	
    38	### 2.1 Definition (per sample, on the metadata dict produced by `AR_md.py`)
    39	
    40	Inputs: `depth` `[3, H, W]` float64 camera-frame point cloud (`convert_equirect_to_camera_coord`, listener at the origin), poses `source [3]`, `source_vit [1,3]`, `context_poses [N,3]`, `context_poses_vit [N,3]`; `W = 512`, `H = 256`.
    41	
    42	**Step A — exact, rotation-invariant depth recovery.** `D[h, w] = depth[2, h, w] / (−sin φ_h)`, `φ_h = (h + 0.5)·π/H − π/2` built exactly as in `AR_md.py` (`torch.arange(H)` → float32 angles → `torch.sin`), cast to `depth.dtype` only at the division. *Why not the L2 norm:* the reviewed yaw operator `rotate_scene_metadata` rolls columns and rotates the vectors with a float32-built matrix, so the per-pixel norm changes at the 1e-7 level and ties/near-ties flip under rotation (probe: 300/300 mismatches). The z channel is bitwise unchanged by a rotation about z (matrix row `[0,0,1]`) and `sin φ_h` is a per-row constant, so `D` after un-rolling equals the unrotated `D` **bitwise** (probe: 0/300 mismatches; `min_h |sin φ_h| = 0.00614`, never 0; `D` matches the raw `.npy` to 8.9e-16).
    43	
    44	**Step B — column profile, deepest column.** `P[w] = max_h D[h, w]`, `M = max_w P[w]`, candidates `C = {w : P[w] == M}` (exact equality on the invariant values).
    45	
    46	**Step C — tie-break (decision D1; recommended Option B).** `|C| = 1` → `j* = C[0]`. Else `j*` = the candidate whose circular profile `(P[c], P[c+1 mod W], …, P[c+W−1 mod W])` is lexicographically **largest** (exact float comparisons; equivariant because the circular profile is a rotation-invariant object read from a candidate anchor). If two candidates have identical circular profiles (the panorama is exactly periodic), choose the one minimizing the wrapped relative source azimuth `(atan2(y_s, x_s) − θ_c) mod 2π`, `θ_c = (c + 0.5)·2π/W − π`; if the source is degenerate (`r_s < 1e-6`) or that is still tied, the smallest `c` — the only non-equivariant residual, which requires an exactly periodic panorama with a symmetric source; its occurrence is counted and written to the metrics record (`canonicalize_residual_ties`). *Option A (literal rule, for comparison):* `j* = argmax_{(h,w)} D` with first-index tie-break — not equivariant for the 3.75 % of panoramas with cross-column ties (frame jumps 0.7°–80°; ≈ 240 of the 6,337 unseen items would fail the invariance test by construction).
    47	
    48	**Step D — the shift.** `dj = (W − j*) mod W`, `α = dj·2π/W`, `md_canon = rotate_scene_metadata(md, α, W)` — the reviewed exp_02 operator (`torch.roll(depth, dj, dims=2)` puts column `j*` at column 0; depth vectors and all four pose fields rotated by `R_z(α)`; `context_audio`, `padding_mask`, `scene`, and the target RIR untouched — a rigid yaw leaves the RIR unchanged). **Post-condition asserted in-loop, fail-closed:** recomputing Steps A–C on `md_canon` yields `j* = 0`.
    49	
    50	**Exactness statement (what "the model sees the same image" means).** For any integer roll `d`, `canon(rot_d(md))` and `canon(md)` have the *identical column arrangement* (integer arithmetic, exact) and vector/pose values equal up to the composition `R(dj')·R(d)` vs `R(dj)` of float32 rotation matrices (≤ ~1e-6 relative). bf16 conditioning autocast then rounds most of that away; the residual is measured by `ε_cond` and by `Δ_C4`/`Δ_R`, never assumed. (A bitwise-exact variant — rebuild the point cloud from the invariant `D` instead of composing rotations — is possible for `depth` but not for the poses; it is NOT proposed: it would duplicate `AR_md.py`'s grid construction and the gain is below bf16 resolution. Reviewer may disagree → D1 sub-option.)
    51	
    52	Idempotence: a second application computes `dj = 0` and returns tensors equal (`torch.equal`) to its input.
    53	
    54	### 2.2 Where it is applied
    55	
